response_text:
Short answer: **don’t apply a Pauli-exclusion-pressure correction**. For a macroscopic copper rod at room temperature and 1 atm, there is no separate post-measurement correction of that kind; adding one would be double-counting physics already built into the material and into textbook values.

The “Pauli pressure” of Cu conduction electrons is the degeneracy pressure of a Fermi sea. For Cu, roughly

\[
n \sim 8.5\times 10^{28}\ \mathrm{m^{-3}},\quad E_F \sim 7\ \mathrm{eV},\quad
P_0=\frac{2}{5}nE_F\sim 4\times 10^{10}\ \mathrm{Pa}.
\]

That sounds huge, but it is **not an external pressure applied to the rod**. It is an internal part of the equilibrium electronic/ionic equation of state, balanced by electron-ion attraction, lattice stresses, Coulomb and exchange-correlation terms. The rod as a whole is in mechanical equilibrium at about 1 atm. The lattice constant, band structure, electron heat capacity, scattering rates, and thermal conductivity of real copper already include Fermi-Dirac statistics and Pauli exclusion.

Also, at room temperature \(T/T_F \sim 300/80{,}000 \sim 4\times 10^{-3}\). Finite-temperature changes in the electron degeneracy pressure are of order \((T/T_F)^2\sim 10^{-5}\). Related thermoelectric/open-circuit corrections to electronic thermal conductivity are of order \(ZT=S^2/L_0\sim 10^{-4}\) for copper. These are far too small to explain a 5% deficit.

So the correction factor for “Pauli pressure” in your data reduction is effectively **1.000**.

A 5% low value is much more likely due to ordinary experimental or material issues, such as:

- heat loss by convection, radiation, supports, thermometer leads, or heater leads;
- poor thermal contact or unaccounted contact/bypass resistances;
- thermometer calibration or spacing errors;
- not reaching steady state;
- uncertainty in cross-sectional area or effective length;
- non-1D heat flow near ends;
- sample purity/grade: OFHC Cu, electrolytic tough-pitch Cu, cold-worked Cu, and alloys can differ by several percent;
- using a textbook value at a different temperature; Cu’s \(\kappa\) is temperature dependent.

If you are doing a first-principles transport calculation, the correct place for Pauli physics is the Fermi-Dirac distribution, band structure, and Pauli blocking in the scattering integral—not an additive pressure correction to the measured thermal conductivity. For your measurement, focus on heat-loss/guarding, calibration, and sample characterization.
